2. What will be output (in general) when you type the following commands into the console? You probably will not certain files available so just describe what the command does. 

a. ls | wc -l
# ls lists one non-hidden entry per line when piped
# wc -l counts those lines and prints the total

b. who | wc -l
# who lists current login sessions, one per line.
# wc -l counts those lines and prints the total number of sessions

c. ls *.c | wc -l
# List filenames ending in .c, one per line, and print the number of matches

d. who | sort
# List current login sessions in alphabetical order by username

e. cp memo1 ..
# Copy memo1 to the parent directory

f. rm ???
# Delete files with exactly three characters in their names

g. mv progs/* /users/steve/backup
# Move all non-hidden files and subdirectories inside progs to /users/steve/backup.

h. rm *.o
# Delete non-hidden files in the current directory whose names end in .o.

g. cd; pwd
# cd with no arguments takes you to your home directory
# The semicolon separates the two commands so they run in order
# pwd prints the full path of the current directory

h. plotdata 2>errors &
# Run plotdata in the background and send its error messages to the errors file.

3. What do the following expressions mean?

a. x*
# zero or more occurences of x

b. xx* 
# one x followed by zero or more occurrences of x

c. x\{1,5\} 
# Match x repeated at least once and at most 5 times

d. x\{5,\} 
# Match 5 or more consecutive occurrences of x, with no upper limit

e. x\{10\} 
# Match exactly 10 consecutive occurrences of x

g. [0,9] 
# Match one character: 0, a comma, or 9

h. [0,9][0,9][0,9]
# Match three consecutive characters, each of which can be 0, 9, or a comma

i. [0-9]\{3\} 
# Match exactly three consecutive digits, each from 0 through 9

j. [0-9]\{3,5\} 
# Match three to five consecutive digits, each from 0 through 9

k. [0-9]\{1,3\},[0-9]\{3\}
# Match one to three digits, followed by a comma, then exactly three digits

l. ^\...
# Match a period followed by any two characters at the start of the line

m. [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9]* 
# Match a letter or underscore followed by zero or more letters, underscores, or digits.

n. \([A-Za-z0-9]\{1,\}\)\1 
# Match one or more letters or digits, followed immediately by the same sequence

o. ^Begin$ 
# Match a line that contains exactly Begin

p. ^\(.\).*\1$ 
# Match a line whose first and last characters are the same with zero or more characters in between

4. What do the following commands output?

a. who | grep 'mary' 
# List current login sessions and print only the lines that contain mary

b. who | grep '^mary ' 
# List current login sessions and print only the lines that start with mary followed by a space

c. grep '[Uu]nix' ch?/* 
# Search non-hidden files directly inside directories named ch plus exactly one character
# Print lines containing Unix or unix

d. ls -l | sort +4n
# List non-hidden files and directories in long format
# Sort numerically by the fifth field (size in bytes), smallest first

e. sed '/^$/d' text > text.out
# Remove empty lines from text and write the result to text.out

f. sed 's/\([Uu]nix\)/\1(TM)/g' text > text.out 
# Add (TM) after every occurrence of Unix or unix in text
# Preserve the original capitalization and write the result to text.out

g. date | cut -c12-16 
# Print only the characters at positions 12 through 16 in the date output

h. date | cut -c5-11,25- | sed 's/\([0-9]\{1,2\}\)/\1,/'
# date prints for example: (Thu Nov 19 16:21:40 PST 2026)
# cut keeps characters 5–11 and 25 through the end (Nov 19 2026)
# sed matches the first one or two consecutive digits (19)
# \1 keeps those digits, and the comma goes immediately after
# Final output is Nov 19, 2026

